----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, P: d7-d5, P: d7-d6, K: e7-d6, K: e7-d8, K: e7-f6, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: Let me analyze this position. I'm Black and White has a White Knight aggressively placed on c7, which is deep in my territory and attacking several key squares. White also has another Knight on f3. My king on e7 is somewhat exposed and restricted.\n\nKey observations:\n1. White's Knight on c7 is a major threat - it's attacking a8 (my rook), controlling important squares, and restricting my pieces\n2. My king on e7 is relatively safe but limited in mobility\n3. I need to develop my pieces and address the Knight on c7\n4. Moving my King to d6 or d8 could help, but doesn't address the immediate threats\n5. Developing my Knights (b8-a6 or b8-c6) would be useful\n6. Pawn moves could help gain space\n\nStrong candidate moves:\n- **N: b8-a6**: Develops the Knight and attacks the invading Knight on c7. This is tactically strong as it challenges White's advanced piece.\n- **N: b8-c6**: Develops the Knight to a central square, but doesn't directly attack c7\n- **K: e7-d6**: Centralizes the King but seems passive\n- **P: d7-d5**: Gains central space but doesn't address immediate threats\n\nThe Knight move to a6 is particularly appealing because it:\n1. Develops a piece\n2. Directly attacks the annoying Knight on c7\n3. Forces White to deal with the threat\n4. Opens potential for my pieces to coordinate better\n\nReason: